<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transition from Sui to Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/613">613</a>–<a href="/years/628">628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The transition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> to Tang was the period of rebellion and civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 613 to 628, as the Sui dynasty collapsed under the burden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>'s failed wars against Goguryeo and his great building projects. Many rival leaders fought for power; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> in 618, and by 628 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, Emperor Taizong, had reunified China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transition-from-sui-to-tang.jpg" alt="Transition from Sui to Tang" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sui_Dynasty_Uprisings.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 611 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> had known more than two decades of peace since ending the Chen dynasty in 589, and both the Eastern Göktürks and Goguryeo were its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. When King Yeongyang of Goguryeo refused to pay homage in 610, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> planned its conquest. Building a fleet and shipping supplies to Zhuo Commandery, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, disrupted farming and killed many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, and from 611 men who refused service rose as agrarian rebels under leaders such as Wang Bo and Liu Badao.</p><p>The campaign of 612 failed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and at the hands of the Goguryeo general Ŭlchi Mundŏk, with the loss of about 300,000 men. During the second campaign, in 613, the general Yang Xuangan rebelled near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>; he was defeated, but the emperor's heavy-handed reprisals did not stop further revolts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 616 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> went to Jiangdu, and the rebels near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> gathered under Li Mi, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> grew strong north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. Several leaders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> among them, submitted to the Eastern Göktürk ruler Ashina Duojishi and received military aid. In winter 617 Li Yuan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and made Yang You emperor. In spring 618 officers of the Xiaoguo Army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-huaji">Yuwen Huaji</a> killed Emperor Yang; Li Yuan then executed Yang You and founded the Tang, while at Luoyang Wang Shichong made himself regent and in 619 took the throne, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, the Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, did much of the fighting. He destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengao">Xue Rengao</a>'s Qin state, drove out Liu Wuzhou by summer 620 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. In spring 621 he defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> at the Hulao Pass, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shichong">Wang Shichong</a> surrendered; the same year Li Xiaogong received the surrender of Xiao Xian's Liang state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a>. Liu Heita, who rose in the former Xia lands, was executed in 623, and Fu Gongshi's Song and Gao Kaidao's Yan were absorbed in 624.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liang-shidu">Liang Shidu</a>, protected by the Eastern Göktürks, held out longest. In 626 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> ambushed and killed his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a>, the Crown Prince, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanji">Li Yuanji</a>, and forced his father to yield the throne to him. By 628 the Eastern Göktürks were divided by disagreements between the Jiali Khan Ashina Duobi and the Tuli Khan Ashina Shibobi and could no longer shield Liang Shidu, whose cousin Liang Luoren killed him and surrendered. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was again under a single power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transition from Sui to Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
